/* The origin of the Snow War (Nov 16, 2026 – Nov 15, 2029), shown by the Origin Story mode.
 * Built by stories/build_origin.py from Jill's doc "How the Snow War Started"
 * (English and Yoopanese sections). An invented story, not a real event. */
window.ORIGIN_STORY = {"en": "<div class=\"os-title\">How the Snow War Started</div>\n<p>The snow war started on a Tulsa block on Thursday, December 10, 2026, when a two-inch snowball of grass and grit missed from twenty feet. It peaked the next day, Friday, December 11, at Skirmish. Everything before that was fought on paper, because there was no snow.</p>\n<p>Each day is scored on its own event, and that event&#39;s depth sets the tier on the Snow War ladder.</p>\n<h3 id=\"os-en-the-paper-war-nov-16-dec-7\">The paper war (Nov 16 – Dec 7)</h3>\n<div class=\"os-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Snow</th><th>Tier</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mon Nov 16</td><td>Bare, low 38°F</td><td>No Ammo</td><td>On the porch, the older siblings disagree about the last war. The 14-year-old says three winters ago, 22 kids, a fort as tall as the mailbox. The 16-year-old says five winters ago, 9 kids, no fort, and it ended because someone cried.</td></tr><tr><td>Tue Nov 17</td><td>Frost, 0.000004&quot;, gone by 8:10 am</td><td>Sketch on Graph Paper</td><td>The 11-year-old draws a machine at four squares to the inch: a screw in a trough, two wheels, a handle, and an arrow labelled &quot;snow goes here.&quot; The left wheel is drawn bigger than the right. It is dated 11/17/26 in felt-tip.</td></tr><tr><td>Fri Nov 20</td><td>Frost, 0.00005&quot;, low 29°F</td><td>Dimensioned Drawing</td><td>The drawing gets numbers: an 11 cm screw, a 7 cm pitch, and a 3.75 cm flight so an acorn can pass. It is checked twice. The left wheel is still bigger.</td></tr><tr><td>Mon Nov 23</td><td>0.0002&quot;</td><td>Screw Pitch Argument</td><td>The 11-year-old and a 10-year-old from two doors down argue for 3 hours over six turns per wheel turn or eight. Six wins, because a walking pace already moves 1.5 litres a second. Neither goes outside to look at the ground.</td></tr><tr><td>Wed Nov 25</td><td>0.0007&quot;</td><td>Cardboard Mock-Up</td><td>The day before Thanksgiving, no school. A delivery box becomes a 30 × 42 × 15 cm shell that fits a 19-litre backpack. Set down on a damp driveway, it falls apart.</td></tr><tr><td>Sat Nov 28</td><td>0.0016&quot;</td><td>Gear Train Laid Out</td><td>Holiday weekend. A ring gear in each hub, a pinion on the screw shaft at 6:1, and a clutch that slips before it can pinch a finger. Every number comes from a catalogue.</td></tr><tr><td>Mon Nov 30</td><td>0.0035&quot;</td><td>First Printed Screw</td><td>Nine hours on a neighbor&#39;s printer make the screw in two glued halves. The seam sticks out half a millimetre and the shaft hole is oval. It turns in the hand.</td></tr><tr><td>Wed Dec 2</td><td>0.007&quot;</td><td>Trough Fit Check</td><td>Clearance is measured at 8 points: 2 to 5.5 mm against a 3 mm target. Forty minutes of filing, and the check is recorded as passed.</td></tr><tr><td>Fri Dec 4</td><td>6 minutes of flurries at 4:15 pm, 0.014&quot;, 31°F</td><td>Clutch Bench Test</td><td>The first flakes of the season, and nobody goes outside. The clutch is tested on a kitchen scale 31 times and releases within 8% every time. It is the first measured number in the project.</td></tr><tr><td>Mon Dec 7</td><td>0.026&quot;</td><td>Dry Run, No Load</td><td>The finished machine is pushed down the hallway 11 times with an empty trough. It clicks. The 11-year-old is told twice to take it outside.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"os-en-the-arctic-week-dec-8-11\">The arctic week (Dec 8 – 11)</h3>\n<p>An arctic front passes Monday night, December 7. Highs stay below freezing and lows run 18–24°F. Three weak clippers follow, each a little wetter than the last, and the cold keeps it Snowball snow, which packs at full value.</p>\n<div class=\"os-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Snow</th><th>Tier</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tue Dec 8</td><td>Flurries 1–3 pm, 0.04&quot;</td><td>Flour Trial</td><td>Still too thin to use, so the machine is tested indoors with 2 kg of flour. It lays a band 28 cm wide and 4 mm deep across 9 feet of kitchen floor. The machine works. The kitchen takes two days to clean, and the trial is never repeated.</td></tr><tr><td>Wed Dec 9</td><td>Snow showers 11 am – 2 pm, 0.05&quot;, 26°F</td><td>A Pinch</td><td>The first throws. The two founders scrape 12 feet of porch railing for 6 minutes and get one pea-sized ball each. Both fall apart within 4 feet. Nobody is hit.</td></tr><tr><td>Thu Dec 10</td><td>0.22&quot; overnight, 22°F at dawn</td><td>One Bad Snowball</td><td>The war starts. The 10-year-old scrapes a whole windshield into a 2-inch ball of mostly grass and grit and throws it from 20 feet at the 11-year-old. It misses by about a yard, and there is nothing left for a second ball. At 3 pm the forecast firms up at 0.5&quot; for Thursday night, and five more kids sign up between the bus stop and dinner.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fri Dec 11</strong></td><td><strong>0.55&quot;, first flakes 9:40 pm Thu, ends 5:50 am Fri, 24°F</strong></td><td><strong>Skirmish (the peak)</strong></td><td>School is open, so the fight starts at 3:25 pm. Seven kids, 4 against 3, with the older siblings watching from the porch and refusing to say who would have won &quot;back then.&quot; About a dozen 3-inch balls per side, packed in 9 minutes. The throwing lasts 1 minute 50 seconds: 24 throws, 6 hits, at about 25 feet. Both sides run out before anyone builds cover, so nobody wins. The machine stays in the garage.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"os-en-the-descent-dec-12-13\">The descent (Dec 12 – 13)</h3>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Sat Dec 12:</strong> sunny, high 41°F. About 0.1&quot; is left on lawns by dusk and none on pavement. Two kids try to pack the leftovers and get slush.</p></li><li><p><strong>Sun Dec 13:</strong> high 47°F, bare ground, back to No Ammo. The graph-paper drawing goes in a desk drawer.</p></li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"os-en-ledger\">Ledger</h3>\n<div class=\"os-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Measure</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>First frost to peak</td><td>25 days</td></tr><tr><td>Events scored</td><td>13</td></tr><tr><td>Total snowfall (Dec 4 and Dec 8–11)</td><td>0.874&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Biggest single event</td><td>0.55&quot; (Skirmish)</td></tr><tr><td>Kids at the peak</td><td>7, plus 2 older siblings watching</td></tr><tr><td>Throws attempted / hits</td><td>26 / 6</td></tr><tr><td>Machine runs on real snow</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>Machine runs on flour</td><td>1, never repeated</td></tr><tr><td>Peak</td><td>Friday, December 11, 2026</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The only dated record of how it began is the felt-tip 11/17/26 in the corner of the drawing.</p>\n<h3 id=\"os-en-the-rest-of-the-winter-jan-7-feb-12\">The rest of the winter (Jan 7 – Feb 12)</h3>\n<p>After New Year a stuck pattern sends a weak system through every three or four days, each a little wetter than the last. Ten more events add 17.28&quot;, and the three biggest come at the end: 2.1&quot; on Feb 2, 2.5&quot; on Feb 5, and 4.5&quot; on Friday, February 12. The winter finishes at 18.15&quot; over 15 snowfalls, about double Tulsa&#39;s normal. All of it is Snowball snow, and each event is scored on its own.</p>\n<div class=\"os-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Snow</th><th>Tier</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Thu Jan 7</td><td>0.4&quot;, 2–6 pm, 30°F</td><td>Handful</td><td>Four kids after school, 7 balls in all, carried in one mitten. Rationing at 2 throws a minute lasts 90 seconds. That evening the drawing comes out of the desk drawer.</td></tr><tr><td>Mon Jan 11</td><td>0.74&quot; overnight, 25°F</td><td>Neighbourhood Loan</td><td>A family that moved onto the block over the holidays has printed a second machine over the weekend from a copy of the files, so for the first time there are two, and both run on real snow. One is lent to a house four doors down on the condition it comes back the same day. It does, three hours late, with grit in the trough and a wing hinge sprung. Four more households ask for one before Friday and are told there are two in existence.</td></tr><tr><td>Fri Jan 15</td><td>0.9&quot;, 4–9 am, 30°F</td><td>Duel</td><td>The 11-year-old and the 12-year-old from the new family write rules on an index card: 30 feet, nothing above the neck, 20 rounds each. Five kids watch from the porch steps. The newcomer wins 5–3 in 10 minutes.</td></tr><tr><td>Mon Jan 18</td><td>1.14&quot;, ends 9 am, 28°F</td><td>Spreader Patent Dispute</td><td>MLK Day, no school. After a morning fight, the new family claims the second machine&#39;s design as its own: 11 cm screw, 7 cm pitch, clutch set to the kitchen-scale figure. The case is heard in a garage by three older kids, the two siblings from the porch and a 13-year-old from the corner. The graph-paper drawing is entered as evidence, dated 11/17/26, left wheel still bigger. The panel rules for the 11-year-old and lets the new family keep printing, as long as every trough carries a scratched mark crediting the design.</td></tr><tr><td>Fri Jan 22</td><td>1.3&quot;, 6 am – 1 pm, 29°F</td><td>Printed in a Basement</td><td>Early release from school. Production moves to the newcomer&#39;s basement, where a printer runs unattended overnight at 11 hours a machine, four a week if nothing warps. The bottleneck stops being design and becomes filament. The printer belongs to the newcomer&#39;s 15-year-old sibling, who has never thrown a snowball in the war and is now the most important person on the block.</td></tr><tr><td>Tue Jan 26</td><td>1.7&quot; overnight, 27°F</td><td>Pitched Battle</td><td>Two-hour late start. After school, both sides fight from fixed positions at 6 to 8 throws a minute, half of them slung off a full-size snow shovel to 55 feet against a hand throw&#39;s 35. Hits are called aloud, about every third one is disputed, and the score at dark is 41–38.</td></tr><tr><td>Sat Jan 30</td><td>2.0&quot; overnight, 30°F</td><td>Spreader Sabotage</td><td>Overnight, someone pours gravel into a machine left out in a driveway. The clutch slips on the first push and nothing breaks, but the trough has to be emptied by hand and the morning&#39;s resurfacing never happens. Nobody admits it. It is the first attack on equipment instead of people.</td></tr><tr><td>Tue Feb 2</td><td>2.1&quot;, 4 am – noon, 28°F</td><td>First Wall</td><td>Groundhog Day, and schools close. The first real wall goes up where the piles stood: 20 inches high, 10 feet long and 8 inches thick, packed by hand in 40 minutes. It stops a direct throw from 15 feet but not a lob.</td></tr><tr><td>Fri Feb 5</td><td>2.5&quot; overnight, 26°F</td><td>Fort Building</td><td>Two-hour late start. After school, two U-shaped forts go up with 30-inch walls a foot thick, 16 feet across the front and 10 feet down each side, about 250 rounds piled inside each. Eleven kids in teams of 5 and 6. The rules go on an index card: no ice, no faces, nobody inside the other fort.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fri Feb 12</strong></td><td><strong>4.5&quot;, first flakes 6:20 pm Thu, ends 7:40 am Fri, 23°F</strong></td><td><strong>Fortress &amp; Siege (the peak)</strong></td><td>Schools close. The Feb 5 forts, slumped to a foot over the week, are rebuilt to 3 feet by noon, each holding 6 with 3 in reserve: 18 kids. By 2 pm both sides make rounds as fast as they throw them, about 900 a side, and the ground between the forts goes unclaimed for 40 minutes at a time. All four machines resurface behind the walls all day. At dark, 6:03 pm, both forts are standing and nobody has won.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"os-en-the-second-descent-feb-13-17\">The second descent (Feb 13 – 17)</h3>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Sat Feb 13:</strong> high 34°F. Twelve kids repair walls in the morning and throw for about 20 minutes after lunch.</p></li><li><p><strong>Sun Feb 14:</strong> sunny, high 44°F. The walls slump to 18 inches.</p></li><li><p><strong>Mon Feb 15:</strong> Presidents&#39; Day, high 53°F, and 0.3&quot; of rain after dark. By morning, only the fort footings are left.</p></li><li><p><strong>Wed Feb 17:</strong> The last footing melts, back to No Ammo. All four machines are hosed off and parked in the garage, and the drawing goes back in the desk drawer.</p></li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"os-en-season-ledger\">Season ledger</h3>\n<div class=\"os-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Measure</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Snowfalls, whole winter</td><td>15 (5 in December, 10 after)</td></tr><tr><td>Snow in December (Dec 4 and Dec 8–11)</td><td>0.874&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Snow, Jan 7 – Feb 12</td><td>17.28&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Season total</td><td>18.15&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Biggest event</td><td>4.5&quot; on Fri Feb 12 (Fortress &amp; Siege)</td></tr><tr><td>Second biggest</td><td>2.5&quot; on Fri Feb 5 (Fort Building)</td></tr><tr><td>Third biggest</td><td>2.1&quot; on Tue Feb 2 (First Wall)</td></tr><tr><td>Kids at the peak</td><td>18</td></tr><tr><td>Machines</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Rounds made at the peak</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>Peak</td><td>Friday, February 12, 2027</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"os-en-after-the-snow-feb-18-nov-15-2027\">After the snow (Feb 18 – Nov 15, 2027)</h3>\n<p>The 4.5&quot; on February 12 was the last snow of the winter. Every day after it scores No Ammo, so this part has no tiers.</p>\n<div class=\"os-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Wed Mar 3</td><td>A 20% chance of flurries. Four kids check the lawn at 7 am, the low is 33°F, and nothing falls. It is the last snow forecast of the winter.</td></tr><tr><td>Sat Mar 13</td><td>The new family asks to keep two machines over the summer. The garage panel meets again for 20 minutes and rules that the files were licensed, not the machines. Then it splits them 2 and 2 anyway, since each household printed two.</td></tr><tr><td>Sun Mar 21</td><td>First day of spring, 71°F. The 11-year-old loads 40 pounds of play sand into a machine. It lays a band 28 cm wide for 3 metres and jams. Like the flour trial, it is never repeated.</td></tr><tr><td>Thu May 20</td><td>Last day of school. The 1–0 on the garage wall, asterisk and all, is still there.</td></tr><tr><td>Tue Jun 1</td><td>A second drawing goes on graph paper at four squares to the inch: Mark II, with both wheels the same size for the first time. It is dated 6/1/27 in felt-tip.</td></tr><tr><td>Thu Aug 12</td><td>First day of school. None of the four machines has moved since March, and one clutch spring has rusted stiff.</td></tr><tr><td>Mon Nov 1</td><td>First frost of the fall, 31°F, gone by 9 am. The 15-year-old orders two spools of filament.</td></tr><tr><td>Mon Nov 15</td><td>Bare, low 39°F, No Ammo. On the porch, the two founders, a year older, disagree about last winter for a kid who just moved in. One says 18 kids and 900 rounds a side. The other says 22 kids and thousands. Neither mentions the gravel.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"os-en-winter-two-nov-16-2027-mar-31-2028\">Winter two (Nov 16, 2027 – Mar 31, 2028)</h3>\n<p>Winter two barely happens. A ridge parks over the Southwest in November and keeps the storm track north all season, so Tulsa gets 0.41&quot; of rain from December through February and 0.004&quot; of snow, all of it in one 25-minute flurry that never sticks. Dry days score the frost and build tiers, and the peak is Rumored on Friday, January 21, 2028.</p>\n<div class=\"os-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Snow</th><th>Tier</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tue Nov 23</td><td>0, 74°F</td><td>No Ammo</td><td>Two days before Thanksgiving. Burn-ban signs go up along the highway; it last rained Oct 29.</td></tr><tr><td>Thu Dec 9</td><td>Rime frost, 0.00004&quot;</td><td>Dimensioned Drawing</td><td>Mark II gets numbers for the first time, in pen: 12 cm screw, 7 cm pitch, both wheels 15 cm. The 12-year-old who drew it checks them twice and finds them consistent, which is more than can be said for the weather.</td></tr><tr><td>Tue Dec 14</td><td>0, 79°F</td><td>No Ammo</td><td>A record high. Six kids play catch with a tennis ball in T-shirts and call it training.</td></tr><tr><td>Sat Dec 25</td><td>0, 66°F</td><td>No Ammo</td><td>No snow on Christmas. The 15-year-old prints Mark II over the break: 31 hours of printer time and two warped troughs, finished Dec 30.</td></tr><tr><td>Wed Jan 12</td><td>Frost, 0.0006&quot;</td><td>Cardboard Mock-Up</td><td>A cereal-box hopper is taped onto Mark II and filled with frost scraped off a windshield. It holds a tablespoon and comes apart when set down on the wet driveway.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fri Jan 21</strong></td><td><strong>Flurries 10:15–10:40 am, 31°F, 0.004&quot;, none sticks</strong></td><td><strong>Rumored (the peak)</strong></td><td>Three kids see it from the gym window at recess. By the bus ride home, 14 say they saw it and one claims to have packed some. Nobody can show a photo, nobody throws anything, and the machines stay in the garage.</td></tr><tr><td>Fri Feb 18</td><td>0, humidity 11%</td><td>No Ammo</td><td>Mark II&#39;s only run of the winter: 40 feet across a dead lawn. It picks up three leaves and a bottle cap.</td></tr><tr><td>Tue Feb 29</td><td>0, 81°F</td><td>No Ammo</td><td>Leap day. Three months, 0.41&quot; of rain, and the driest winter anyone on the porch can remember.</td></tr><tr><td>Fri Mar 31</td><td>0</td><td>No Ammo</td><td>Winter ends. Season snow 0.004&quot;, none of it on the ground for more than a second.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"os-en-after-winter-two-apr-1-nov-15-2028\">After winter two (Apr 1 – Nov 15, 2028)</h3>\n<p>No snow falls, so this part has no tiers. The drought runs into summer, the fleet grows anyway, and the year ends on the porch.</p>\n<div class=\"os-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sat Apr 8</td><td>1.6&quot; of rain, the first real rain in nine weeks. The burn ban lifts. The machines get hosed down anyway, though none has touched snow since Feb 12, 2027.</td></tr><tr><td>Fri May 19</td><td>Last day of school. The 1–0 on the garage wall is still there, asterisk and all.</td></tr><tr><td>Thu Jun 1</td><td>Mark III goes on graph paper at four squares to the inch: twin screws, a 40 cm swath, wheels the same size again. It is dated 6/1/28 in felt-tip. A drawing every June 1 is now a tradition.</td></tr><tr><td>Sat Jul 22</td><td>108°F. Nobody goes outside after 11 am.</td></tr><tr><td>Mon Aug 14</td><td>First day of school. A 10-year-old moves into the corner house and asks about the war. The founders give two different years.</td></tr><tr><td>Sat Oct 14</td><td>The newcomer&#39;s sibling, now 16, finishes the third Mark III at 46 hours of printer time each. The block now has 8 machines: 4 Mark I, 1 Mark II, 3 Mark III.</td></tr><tr><td>Wed Nov 1</td><td>First frost, 30°F, gone by 9 am. Two more spools of filament are ordered.</td></tr><tr><td>Wed Nov 15</td><td>Bare, low 41°F, No Ammo. On the porch, the two founders, now 13, disagree about winter two for the 10-year-old. One says it snowed on Jan 21. The other says it was lint from the gym vent. The 10-year-old asks what a spreader is and gets 40 minutes.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"os-en-winter-three-nov-21-2028-apr-7-2029\">Winter three (Nov 21, 2028 – Apr 7, 2029)</h3>\n<p>Winter three never lets up. A cold trough sets up over the eastern half of the country in mid-November and holds until late March, sending a clipper or a storm through every four or five days. Twenty-four snowfalls add 76.35&quot;, about nine times Tulsa&#39;s normal. Six are heavy storms of 4&quot; or more, and the five midwinter ones each beat the last, up to 14.0&quot; on Friday, February 16, 2029. The ground stays white for 112 days. All of it is Snowball snow, and each event is scored on its own, so a clipper on top of two feet of pack still scores only its own inches.</p>\n<div class=\"os-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Snow</th><th>Tier</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tue Nov 21</td><td>0.62&quot;, 5–9 am, 31°F</td><td>Flanking Run</td><td>First snow in 21 months, two days before Thanksgiving. About 18 rounds a side. The 10-year-old circles 60 feet in 11 seconds and closes to 12 feet before anyone looks. It works once. Gone by 2 pm.</td></tr><tr><td>Mon Nov 27</td><td>1.4&quot; overnight, 28°F</td><td>Extended Engagement</td><td>Two-hour late start. After school, 80 rounds a side last longer than the gloves. Everyone dries out indoors at the 40-minute mark and goes back out.</td></tr><tr><td>Fri Dec 1</td><td>1.2&quot;, 3–8 am, 27°F</td><td>First Snowman</td><td>The ground stays white from today until March 22. A 22-inch snowman goes up before the bus, and by noon someone has scratched the spreader mark into its back.</td></tr><tr><td>Tue Dec 5</td><td>1.6&quot; overnight, 25°F</td><td>Escorted Spreaders</td><td>Nobody has forgotten the gravel. Every Mark III goes out with two guards, and completed resurfacing runs go from under half to nine in ten.</td></tr><tr><td>Sat Dec 9</td><td>4.4&quot;, 9 pm Fri – 11 am Sat, 24°F</td><td>Star Fort</td><td>The first heavy storm. A 12-foot square fort gets a pointed bastion at each corner, walls 3 feet high and 16 inches thick. Four of the nine builders argue the angles with a protractor until dark. Nobody rushes it twice.</td></tr><tr><td>Wed Dec 13</td><td>2.05&quot; overnight, 23°F</td><td>Angel Quarry</td><td>A row of six angels on the side lawn is cut up overnight for 41 rounds. The makers lodge a formal objection, and a rule is passed that no angel may be quarried until it is a day old. Nobody can check.</td></tr><tr><td>Sun Dec 17</td><td>1.38&quot;, 1–6 pm, 26°F</td><td>Angel Truce</td><td>Anyone making an angel is not thrown at. Nine angels in two hours. The rule ends at 4 pm when one maker is found to have been resting in an angel for 22 minutes.</td></tr><tr><td>Thu Dec 21</td><td>6.0&quot;, 4 pm Wed – 10 am Thu, 22°F</td><td>Multi-Fort Campaign</td><td>First day of winter break. Three forts hold 400 feet of frontage across four yards, 30 kids in all. Two of them ally against the third by 1 pm, and the alliance lasts nine days.</td></tr><tr><td>Wed Dec 27</td><td>2.5&quot; overnight, 20°F</td><td>Fort Building</td><td>The Dec 21 forts have glazed and slumped. Three new U-forts go up on the standard pattern, 30-inch walls a foot thick, with 250 rounds inside each and 90 minutes of building before the first throw.</td></tr><tr><td>Mon Jan 1</td><td>1.8&quot;, 2–8 am, 19°F</td><td>Rampart Skim Coat</td><td>New Year&#39;s Day. All 8 machines lay a 2 mm coat over every grey wall on the block. It adds nothing structural, and from 40 feet everything looks new.</td></tr><tr><td>Fri Jan 5</td><td>8.2&quot;, 7 pm Thu – 1 pm Fri, 21°F</td><td>Tunnel Networks</td><td>Schools close. The trenches are linked underground, 3 feet down and 150 feet in all, with four entrances, two of them hidden. Parents are told once it is finished and reinforce it with sled slats every 6 feet.</td></tr><tr><td>Wed Jan 10</td><td>2.2&quot; overnight, 24°F</td><td>Bat and Racquet</td><td>A plastic bat and a warped racquet come out of a garage. A clean hit carries 65 feet, but three rounds in five burst on the swing, so the packing gets harder.</td></tr><tr><td>Mon Jan 15</td><td>2.6&quot;, 4–11 am, 22°F</td><td>Fort-Side Spreader Bay</td><td>MLK Day. Every fort is rebuilt with a bay at the back, sized to a Mark III with its wings folded and roofed with a sled. Turnaround falls under 3 minutes.</td></tr><tr><td>Sat Jan 20</td><td>1.9&quot; overnight, 18°F</td><td>Dug-In Line</td><td>Two lines are scraped 8 inches into the pack, 20 feet long, facing each other at 50 feet. Neither is abandoned all afternoon.</td></tr><tr><td>Thu Jan 25</td><td>10.4&quot;, 3 am Thu – 4 am Fri, 20°F</td><td>Bailey Snowmen</td><td>Schools close Thursday and Friday. The two allied forts share a keep and a 60-foot inner wall, and 14 snowmen, 7 feet tall, go up in the bailey out of reach. Their carrots are replaced every nine days from a list pinned by the door.</td></tr><tr><td>Tue Jan 30</td><td>2.75&quot; overnight, 23°F</td><td>Redoubt</td><td>A fallback position, 6 feet across with 80 rounds, goes up behind each main wall. Both are used twice, and both lines are recovered within ten minutes.</td></tr><tr><td>Mon Feb 5</td><td>3.0&quot;, 6 am – 2 pm, 25°F</td><td>Two Forts</td><td>Early release. The third fort&#39;s side builds a rival square fort 18 feet on a side, 50 feet from the allied front. Fourteen person-hours of building before anyone throws.</td></tr><tr><td>Fri Feb 9</td><td>1.5&quot; overnight, 26°F</td><td>Saucer Heats</td><td>A blue saucer arrives to race the green one. Blue wins seven of twelve over 34 feet, and someone is waiting at the top at all times.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fri Feb 16</strong></td><td><strong>14.0&quot;, first flakes 5:10 pm Thu, ends 11:30 am Fri, 22°F, wind under 10 mph</strong></td><td><strong>Counter-Battery (the peak)</strong></td><td>Schools close. By 8 am the first catapult is built from a sled, a fence post and four bungee cords: 110 feet, then it breaks on the second shot. By 11 am an inner-tube ballista puts one round in four inside a two-foot circle at 130 feet. By 2 pm the engines are being built to destroy other engines, out to 140 feet. Four are in service across three forts, 34 kids, and all 8 machines resurfacing behind the walls. At dark, 6:12 pm, every fort is standing and two engines are down.</td></tr><tr><td>Thu Feb 22</td><td>1.0&quot;, 5–9 am, 27°F</td><td>Free-For-All</td><td>Three of the four engines are disabled within the week, two by other engines. With no engines left, nine kids fight for 15 minutes with no teams, and nobody agrees who won.</td></tr><tr><td>Fri Mar 2</td><td>0.4&quot;, 6–8 am, 31°F</td><td>Handful</td><td>The pack is down to 14 inches and crusted. Fresh snow is rationed to seven rounds carried in one mitten.</td></tr><tr><td>Thu Mar 15</td><td>4.05&quot;, 8 pm Wed – 9 am Thu, 29°F</td><td>Two-Room Fort</td><td>A late heavy storm. The last fort of the winter gets a firing room in front and a store room behind with a doorway between, the first inside division in the war. Arguments are settled in the back room.</td></tr><tr><td>Mon Mar 26</td><td>1.1&quot;, 4–7 am, 32°F</td><td>Snowball Fight</td><td>The ground went bare on Mar 23. Eight kids fight from behind parked cars for 25 minutes, 40 rounds a side, and it is gone by supper.</td></tr><tr><td>Sat Apr 7</td><td>0.3&quot;, 5–6 am, 33°F</td><td>Two Snowballs</td><td>The last snow of the winter, on the back of a car. Two rounds, two throws, one hit. The engagement lasts four seconds.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"os-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Measure</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Snowfalls</td><td>24 (2 Nov, 7 Dec, 7 Jan, 4 Feb, 3 Mar, 1 Apr)</td></tr><tr><td>Heavy storms (4&quot; or more)</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>Season total</td><td>76.35&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Biggest event</td><td>14.0&quot; on Fri Feb 16 (Counter-Battery)</td></tr><tr><td>Second biggest</td><td>10.4&quot; on Thu Jan 25 (Bailey Snowmen)</td></tr><tr><td>Third biggest</td><td>8.2&quot; on Fri Jan 5 (Tunnel Networks)</td></tr><tr><td>Deepest on the ground</td><td>27&quot; on Feb 16</td></tr><tr><td>Days with snow on the ground</td><td>112 in a row (Dec 1 – Mar 22)</td></tr><tr><td>School snow days</td><td>13</td></tr><tr><td>Kids at the peak</td><td>34</td></tr><tr><td>Machines</td><td>8 (4 Mark I, 1 Mark II, 3 Mark III)</td></tr><tr><td>Engines at the peak</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>Peak</td><td>Friday, February 16, 2029</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"os-en-after-winter-three-apr-8-nov-15-2029\">After winter three (Apr 8 – Nov 15, 2029)</h3>\n<p>The 0.3&quot; on April 7 was the last snow of winter three. Every day after it scores No Ammo, so this part has no tiers. It is the year the war gets cleaned up, ruled on, drawn again, and handed down.</p>\n<div class=\"os-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sun Apr 8</td><td>The carrot list comes down off the keep door. The 14 bailey snowmen went through six changes, 98 carrots in all, and the list is filed in the desk drawer with both drawings.</td></tr><tr><td>Sat Apr 14</td><td>Four lawns have brown rectangles where the forts stood for 112 days. One parent reseeds for $63. A sled slat from the tunnels turns up in a downspout.</td></tr><tr><td>Sat Apr 28</td><td>The garage panel sits for the first time on engines. The bungee cords go back to their owners and the fence post goes back into the fence. Nobody can claim the design, because nobody drew one, and the panel rules that an engine without a drawing belongs to no one.</td></tr><tr><td>Fri May 18</td><td>Last day of school. The 1–0 on the garage wall now has a second line under it: &quot;W3: 4 engines, 0 winners.&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Sat May 19</td><td>The newcomer&#39;s sibling, who owns the printer, graduates and will leave for college in August.</td></tr><tr><td>Fri Jun 1</td><td>The June 1 drawing is two sheets this year. One is Mark IV, a spreader that folds to fit a sled. The other is the war&#39;s first engine drawing, a counterweight trebuchet dimensioned to the inch and dated 6/1/29. Nobody builds it.</td></tr><tr><td>Wed Jul 4</td><td>101°F. The fireworks are watched from the spot where the keep stood.</td></tr><tr><td>Sat Aug 11</td><td>The printer moves into the founders&#39; garage with a note taped to it: &quot;Filament in the blue bin. Don&#39;t print the treb in PLA.&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Mon Aug 13</td><td>First day of school. The founders start ninth grade, and the kid who moved into the corner house in 2028, now 11, starts middle school.</td></tr><tr><td>Thu Nov 1</td><td>First frost, 29°F, gone by 10 am. The trebuchet drawing comes out of the drawer for the first time since June.</td></tr><tr><td>Thu Nov 15</td><td>Bare, low 40°F, No Ammo. On the porch, the two founders, now 14, disagree about winter three. One says 34 kids and four engines. The other says 60 kids and a trebuchet. The 11-year-old, who was there for all of it, gets it right and is told they were too young to remember.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n", "yooper": "<div class=\"os-title\">How Da Whole Ting Started</div>\n<p>Da snow war started on a block down in Tulsa on Thursday, December 10, 2026, when a two-inch snowball made outta grass an&#39; grit missed from twenty feet, eh. It peaked da next day, Friday, December 11, at Little Skirmish. Everyt&#39;ing before dat got fought on paper, &#39;cause dere wasn&#39;t no snow.</p>\n<p>Every day gets scored on its own event, an&#39; how deep dat one is sets da tier on da Snow War ladder.</p>\n<h3 id=\"os-yp-da-paper-war-nov-16-dec-7\">Da paper war (Nov 16 – Dec 7)</h3>\n<div class=\"os-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Snow</th><th>Tier</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mon Nov 16</td><td>Bare, low 38°F</td><td>No Ammo, Eh</td><td>Out on da porch, da older brothers an&#39; sisters can&#39;t agree on da last war. Da 14-year-old says t&#39;ree winters back, 22 kids, an&#39; a fort tall as da mailbox. Da 16-year-old says five winters back, 9 kids, no fort, an&#39; it quit &#39;cause somebody started bawlin&#39;.</td></tr><tr><td>Tue Nov 17</td><td>Frost, 0.000004&quot;, gone by 8:10 am</td><td>Sketch on Da Graph Paper</td><td>Da 11-year-old draws a machine at four squares to da inch: a screw in a trough, two wheels, a handle, an&#39; an arrow sayin&#39; &quot;snow goes here.&quot; Da left wheel&#39;s drawn bigger dan da right one. It&#39;s dated 11/17/26 in felt-tip.</td></tr><tr><td>Fri Nov 20</td><td>Frost, 0.00005&quot;, low 29°F</td><td>Drawin&#39; Wit&#39; Numbers</td><td>Da drawin&#39; gets numbers: an 11 cm screw, a 7 cm pitch, an&#39; a 3.75 cm flight so an acorn can get t&#39;rough. It gets checked twice. Da left wheel&#39;s still bigger, yah.</td></tr><tr><td>Mon Nov 23</td><td>0.0002&quot;</td><td>Arguin&#39; Over Da Screw Pitch</td><td>Da 11-year-old an&#39; a 10-year-old from two doors down go at it for t&#39;ree hours over six turns per wheel turn or eight. Six wins, &#39;cause walkin&#39; pace already moves a litre an&#39; a half a second. Neider one goes outside to look at da ground.</td></tr><tr><td>Wed Nov 25</td><td>0.0007&quot;</td><td>Cardboard Mock-Up, Eh</td><td>Day before Thanksgivin&#39;, no school. A delivery box turns into a 30 × 42 × 15 cm shell dat fits a 19-litre backpack. Ya set it down on a wet driveway an&#39; it falls apart.</td></tr><tr><td>Sat Nov 28</td><td>0.0016&quot;</td><td>Gear Train All Laid Out</td><td>Holiday weekend. A ring gear in each hub, a pinion on da screw shaft at 6 to 1, an&#39; a clutch dat slips before it can pinch a finger. Every number comes outta a catalogue.</td></tr><tr><td>Mon Nov 30</td><td>0.0035&quot;</td><td>First Printed Screw</td><td>Nine hours on da neighbor&#39;s printer makes da screw in two glued halves. Da seam sticks out half a millimetre an&#39; da shaft hole&#39;s oval. It turns in yer hand, anyways.</td></tr><tr><td>Wed Dec 2</td><td>0.007&quot;</td><td>Checkin&#39; Da Trough Fit</td><td>Clearance gets measured at 8 spots: 2 to 5.5 mm, an&#39; dey wanted 3. Forty minutes of filin&#39;, an&#39; da check goes down as passed.</td></tr><tr><td>Fri Dec 4</td><td>Six minutes of flurries at 4:15 pm, 0.014&quot;, 31°F</td><td>Clutch on Da Bench</td><td>First flakes of da season, an&#39; nobody even goes out to look. Da clutch gets tested on a kitchen scale 31 times an&#39; lets go wit&#39;in 8% every time. Dat&#39;s da first number in da whole project somebody actually measured.</td></tr><tr><td>Mon Dec 7</td><td>0.026&quot;</td><td>Dry Run, Nuttin&#39; In It</td><td>Da finished machine gets pushed down da hallway 11 times wit&#39; an empty trough. It clicks. Da 11-year-old gets told twice, &quot;Take dat t&#39;ing outside, ya.&quot;</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"os-yp-da-arctic-week-dec-8-11\">Da arctic week (Dec 8 – 11)</h3>\n<p>An arctic front comes t&#39;rough Monday night, December 7. Highs stay below freezin&#39; an&#39; lows run 18 to 24°F. T&#39;ree little clippers follow, each one a bit wetter dan da last, an&#39; da cold keeps it Snowball snow, da kind dat packs full value.</p>\n<div class=\"os-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Snow</th><th>Tier</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tue Dec 8</td><td>Flurries 1–3 pm, 0.04&quot;</td><td>Da Flour Test</td><td>Still too t&#39;in to use, so dey test da machine inside wit&#39; 2 kg of flour. It lays a strip 28 cm wide an&#39; 4 mm deep across 9 feet of kitchen floor. Da machine works. Da kitchen takes two days to clean up, an&#39; nobody ever tries dat again, ya know.</td></tr><tr><td>Wed Dec 9</td><td>Snow showers 11 am – 2 pm, 0.05&quot;, 26°F</td><td>Just a Pinch</td><td>First t&#39;rows. Da two founders scrape 12 feet of porch railin&#39; for six minutes an&#39; get one ball each, about da size of a pea. Bot&#39; of &#39;em fall apart wit&#39;in four feet. Nobody gets hit.</td></tr><tr><td>Thu Dec 10</td><td>0.22&quot; overnight, 22°F at dawn</td><td>One Crummy Snowball</td><td>Da war starts. Da 10-year-old scrapes off a whole windshield, panks it into a 2-inch ball dat&#39;s mostly grass an&#39; grit, an&#39; wings it from 20 feet at da 11-year-old. Misses by about a yard, an&#39; dere&#39;s nuttin&#39; left for anudder one. At 3 pm da forecast firms up at half an inch for Thursday night, an&#39; five more kids sign on between da bus stop an&#39; supper.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fri Dec 11</strong></td><td><strong>0.55&quot;, first flakes 9:40 pm Thu, quits 5:50 am Fri, 24°F</strong></td><td><strong>Little Skirmish (da peak)</strong></td><td>School&#39;s open, so it don&#39;t start till 3:25 pm. Seven kids, 4 against 3, choppers on, wit&#39; da older brothers an&#39; sisters watchin&#39; from da porch an&#39; refusin&#39; to say who woulda won &quot;back den.&quot; About a dozen 3-inch balls a side, panked in nine minutes. Da t&#39;rowin&#39; lasts 1 minute 50 seconds: 24 t&#39;rows, 6 hits, at about 25 feet. Bot&#39; sides run out before anybody builds cover, so nobody wins, eh. Da machine stays in da garage.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"os-yp-da-comedown-dec-12-13\">Da comedown (Dec 12 – 13)</h3>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Sat Dec 12:</strong> sunny, high 41°F. Maybe a tent&#39; of an inch left on da lawns by dark an&#39; nuttin&#39; on da pavement. Two kids try to pank what&#39;s left an&#39; get slush.</p></li><li><p><strong>Sun Dec 13:</strong> high 47°F, bare ground, right back to No Ammo, Eh. Da graph-paper drawin&#39; goes in a desk drawer.</p></li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"os-yp-da-ledger\">Da ledger</h3>\n<div class=\"os-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Measure</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>First frost to da peak</td><td>25 days</td></tr><tr><td>Events scored</td><td>13</td></tr><tr><td>Total snowfall (Dec 4 an&#39; Dec 8–11)</td><td>0.874&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Biggest single event</td><td>0.55&quot; (Little Skirmish)</td></tr><tr><td>Kids at da peak</td><td>7, plus 2 older ones watchin&#39;</td></tr><tr><td>T&#39;rows tried / hits</td><td>26 / 6</td></tr><tr><td>Machine runs on real snow</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>Machine runs on flour</td><td>1, never again</td></tr><tr><td>Peak</td><td>Friday, December 11, 2026</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Da only dated record of how it all started is dat felt-tip 11/17/26 in da corner of da drawin&#39;.</p>\n<h3 id=\"os-yp-da-rest-of-da-winter-jan-7-feb-12\">Da rest of da winter (Jan 7 – Feb 12)</h3>\n<p>After New Year da weather gets stuck an&#39; sends a little system t&#39;rough every t&#39;ree or four days, each one a bit wetter dan da last, eh. Ten more events add 17.28&quot;, an&#39; da t&#39;ree biggest come at da end: 2.1&quot; on Feb 2, 2.5&quot; on Feb 5, an&#39; 4.5&quot; on Friday, February 12. Da winter finishes at 18.15&quot; over 15 snowfalls, about double what Tulsa usually gets. All of it&#39;s Snowball snow, an&#39; every event gets scored on its own.</p>\n<div class=\"os-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Snow</th><th>Tier</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Thu Jan 7</td><td>0.4&quot;, 2–6 pm, 30°F</td><td>A Handful, Yah</td><td>Four kids after school, 7 balls total, an&#39; dey all fit in one chopper. Rationin&#39; at 2 t&#39;rows a minute lasts 90 seconds. Dat night da drawin&#39; comes back outta da desk drawer.</td></tr><tr><td>Mon Jan 11</td><td>0.74&quot; overnight, 25°F</td><td>Lent to Da Neighbours</td><td>A family dat moved onto da block over da holidays printed a second machine over da weekend from a copy of da files, so for da first time dere&#39;s two, an&#39; bot&#39; of &#39;em run on real snow. One gets lent to a house four doors down, long as it comes back da same day. It does, t&#39;ree hours late, wit&#39; grit in da trough an&#39; a wing hinge sprung, eh. Four more households ask for one before Friday an&#39; get told dere&#39;s only two in da whole world.</td></tr><tr><td>Fri Jan 15</td><td>0.9&quot;, 4–9 am, 30°F</td><td>Da Duel</td><td>Da 11-year-old an&#39; da 12-year-old from da new family write da rules on an index card: 30 feet, nuttin&#39; above da neck, 20 rounds each. Five kids watch from da porch steps. Da new kid wins 5–3 in 10 minutes.</td></tr><tr><td>Mon Jan 18</td><td>1.14&quot;, quits 9 am, 28°F</td><td>Spreader Patent Spat</td><td>MLK Day, no school. After a mornin&#39; fight, da new family claims da second machine&#39;s design as deir own: 11 cm screw, 7 cm pitch, clutch set to da kitchen-scale number. Da case gets heard in a garage by t&#39;ree older kids, da two from da porch an&#39; a 13-year-old from da corner. Da graph-paper drawin&#39; goes in as evidence, dated 11/17/26, left wheel still bigger. Da panel rules for da 11-year-old an&#39; lets da new family keep printin&#39;, long as every trough&#39;s got a mark scratched in givin&#39; credit for da design.</td></tr><tr><td>Fri Jan 22</td><td>1.3&quot;, 6 am – 1 pm, 29°F</td><td>Printed Down in Da Basement</td><td>Early release from school. Production moves down to da new family&#39;s basement, where a printer runs all night wit&#39; nobody watchin&#39;, 11 hours a machine, four a week if nuttin&#39; warps. Da holdup ain&#39;t da design no more, it&#39;s filament. Da printer belongs to da 15-year-old in dat family, who&#39;s never t&#39;rown one snowball in da war an&#39; is now da most important person on da block, ya know.</td></tr><tr><td>Tue Jan 26</td><td>1.7&quot; overnight, 27°F</td><td>Pitched Battle</td><td>Two-hour late start. After school bot&#39; sides fight from fixed spots at 6 to 8 t&#39;rows a minute, half of &#39;em slung off a full-size snow shovel to 55 feet, where a hand t&#39;row only gets 35. Hits get called out loud, about every t&#39;ird one gets argued, an&#39; da score at dark is 41–38.</td></tr><tr><td>Sat Jan 30</td><td>2.0&quot; overnight, 30°F</td><td>Spreader Sabotage</td><td>Overnight, somebody dumps gravel into a machine left out in a driveway. Da clutch slips on da first push an&#39; nuttin&#39; breaks, but da trough&#39;s gotta be emptied out by hand an&#39; da mornin&#39; coverin&#39; never happens. Nobody owns up. It&#39;s da first attack on da equipment instead of people.</td></tr><tr><td>Tue Feb 2</td><td>2.1&quot;, 4 am – noon, 28°F</td><td>First Wall</td><td>Groundhog Day, an&#39; school&#39;s closed. Da first real wall goes up where da piles were: 20 inches high, 10 feet long an&#39; 8 inches t&#39;ick, panked by hand in 40 minutes. It stops a straight t&#39;row from 15 feet but not a lob.</td></tr><tr><td>Fri Feb 5</td><td>2.5&quot; overnight, 26°F</td><td>Buildin&#39; Forts</td><td>Two-hour late start. After school two U-shaped forts go up wit&#39; 30-inch walls a foot t&#39;ick, 16 feet across da front an&#39; 10 feet down each side, about 250 rounds piled up inside each one. Eleven kids in teams of 5 an&#39; 6. Da rules go on an index card: no ice, no faces, nobody inside da udder fort.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fri Feb 12</strong></td><td><strong>4.5&quot;, first flakes 6:20 pm Thu, quits 7:40 am Fri, 23°F</strong></td><td><strong>Fortress an&#39; Siege (da peak)</strong></td><td>School&#39;s closed. Da Feb 5 forts, slumped down to a foot over da week, get built back up to 3 feet by noon, each holdin&#39; 6 wit&#39; 3 in reserve: 18 kids. By 2 pm bot&#39; sides make rounds as fast as dey t&#39;row &#39;em, about 900 a side, an&#39; da ground between da forts sits empty for 40 minutes at a time. All four machines keep coverin&#39; behind da walls all day. At dark, 6:03 pm, bot&#39; forts are still standin&#39; an&#39; nobody&#39;s won, eh.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"os-yp-da-second-comedown-feb-13-17\">Da second comedown (Feb 13 – 17)</h3>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Sat Feb 13:</strong> high 34°F. Twelve kids fix up da walls in da mornin&#39; an&#39; t&#39;row for about 20 minutes after lunch.</p></li><li><p><strong>Sun Feb 14:</strong> sunny, high 44°F. Da walls slump down to 18 inches.</p></li><li><p><strong>Mon Feb 15:</strong> Presidents&#39; Day, high 53°F, an&#39; 0.3&quot; of rain after dark. By mornin&#39; only da fort footin&#39;s are left.</p></li><li><p><strong>Wed Feb 17:</strong> da last footin&#39; melts, right back to No Ammo, Eh. All four machines get hosed off an&#39; parked in da garage, an&#39; da drawin&#39; goes back in da desk drawer.</p></li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"os-yp-da-season-ledger\">Da season ledger</h3>\n<div class=\"os-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Measure</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Snowfalls, whole winter</td><td>15 (5 in December, 10 after)</td></tr><tr><td>Snow in December (Dec 4 an&#39; Dec 8–11)</td><td>0.874&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Snow, Jan 7 – Feb 12</td><td>17.28&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Season total</td><td>18.15&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Biggest event</td><td>4.5&quot; on Fri Feb 12 (Fortress an&#39; Siege)</td></tr><tr><td>Second biggest</td><td>2.5&quot; on Fri Feb 5 (Buildin&#39; Forts)</td></tr><tr><td>Third biggest</td><td>2.1&quot; on Tue Feb 2 (First Wall)</td></tr><tr><td>Kids at da peak</td><td>18</td></tr><tr><td>Machines</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Rounds made at da peak</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>Peak</td><td>Friday, February 12, 2027</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"os-yp-after-da-snow-feb-18-nov-15-2027\">After da snow (Feb 18 – Nov 15, 2027)</h3>\n<p>Dat 4.5&quot; on February 12 was da last snow of da winter, eh. Every day after it scores No Ammo, so dis part&#39;s got no tiers.</p>\n<div class=\"os-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Wed Mar 3</td><td>A 20% chance of flurries. Four kids check da lawn at 7 am, da low&#39;s 33°F, an&#39; nuttin&#39; falls. Dat&#39;s da last snow forecast of da winter.</td></tr><tr><td>Sat Mar 13</td><td>Da new family asks to keep two machines over da summer. Da garage panel meets again for 20 minutes an&#39; rules da files got licensed, not da machines. Den dey split &#39;em 2 an&#39; 2 anyways, since each household printed two.</td></tr><tr><td>Sun Mar 21</td><td>First day of spring, 71°F. Da 11-year-old loads 40 pounds of sandbox sand into a machine. It lays a strip 28 cm wide for 3 metres an&#39; jams up. Same as da flour test, nobody ever tries dat again.</td></tr><tr><td>Thu May 20</td><td>Last day of school. Da 1–0 on da garage wall, asterisk an&#39; all, is still up dere.</td></tr><tr><td>Tue Jun 1</td><td>A second drawin&#39; goes on graph paper at four squares to da inch: Mark II, wit&#39; bot&#39; wheels da same size for da first time ever. It&#39;s dated 6/1/27 in felt-tip.</td></tr><tr><td>Thu Aug 12</td><td>First day of school. None of da four machines has moved since March, an&#39; one clutch spring&#39;s rusted stiff.</td></tr><tr><td>Mon Nov 1</td><td>First frost of da fall, 31°F, gone by 9 am. Da 15-year-old orders two spools of filament.</td></tr><tr><td>Mon Nov 15</td><td>Bare, low 39°F, No Ammo, Eh. Out on da porch, da two founders, a year older now, can&#39;t agree on last winter for a kid who just moved in. One says 18 kids an&#39; 900 rounds a side. Da udder says 22 kids an&#39; t&#39;ousands. Neider one brings up da gravel.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"os-yp-winter-two-nov-16-2027-mar-31-2028-1\">Winter two (Nov 16, 2027 – Mar 31, 2028)</h3>\n<p>Winter two barely shows up, eh. A ridge parks over da Southwest in November an&#39; keeps da storms up nort&#39; all season, so Tulsa gets 0.41&quot; of rain from December t&#39;rough February an&#39; 0.004&quot; of snow, all of it in one 25-minute flurry dat never sticks. Da dry days score da frost an&#39; buildin&#39; tiers, an&#39; da peak is Heard It Down at Da Bar on Friday, January 21, 2028.</p>\n<div class=\"os-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Snow</th><th>Tier</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tue Nov 23</td><td>0, 74°F</td><td>No Ammo, Eh</td><td>Two days before T&#39;anksgiving. Burn-ban signs go up along da highway; da last rain was Oct 29.</td></tr><tr><td>Thu Dec 9</td><td>Rime frost, 0.00004&quot;</td><td>Drawin&#39; Wit&#39; Numbers</td><td>Mark II gets numbers for da first time, in pen: 12 cm screw, 7 cm pitch, bot&#39; wheels 15 cm. Da 12-year-old who drew it checks &#39;em twice an&#39; dey add up, which is more dan you can say for da weather.</td></tr><tr><td>Tue Dec 14</td><td>0, 79°F</td><td>No Ammo, Eh</td><td>A record high. Six kids play catch wit&#39; a tennis ball in T-shirts an&#39; call it trainin&#39;.</td></tr><tr><td>Sat Dec 25</td><td>0, 66°F</td><td>No Ammo, Eh</td><td>No snow on Christmas. Da 15-year-old prints Mark II over da break: 31 hours of printer time an&#39; two warped troughs, done Dec 30.</td></tr><tr><td>Wed Jan 12</td><td>Frost, 0.0006&quot;</td><td>Cardboard Mock-Up, Eh</td><td>A cereal-box hopper gets taped onto Mark II an&#39; filled wit&#39; frost scraped off a windshield. It holds a tablespoon an&#39; falls apart when ya set it down on da wet driveway.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fri Jan 21</strong></td><td><strong>Flurries 10:15–10:40 am, 31°F, 0.004&quot;, none sticks</strong></td><td><strong>Heard It Down at Da Bar (da peak)</strong></td><td>T&#39;ree kids see it from da gym window at recess. By da bus ride home, 14 say dey saw it an&#39; one claims to have packed some. Nobody&#39;s got a picture, nobody t&#39;rows nuttin&#39;, an&#39; da machines stay in da garage.</td></tr><tr><td>Fri Feb 18</td><td>0, humidity 11%</td><td>No Ammo, Eh</td><td>Mark II&#39;s only run all winter: 40 feet across a dead lawn. It picks up t&#39;ree leaves an&#39; a bottle cap.</td></tr><tr><td>Tue Feb 29</td><td>0, 81°F</td><td>No Ammo, Eh</td><td>Leap day. T&#39;ree months, 0.41&quot; of rain, an&#39; da driest winter anybody on da porch can remember.</td></tr><tr><td>Fri Mar 31</td><td>0</td><td>No Ammo, Eh</td><td>Winter&#39;s over. Season snow 0.004&quot;, an&#39; none of it stayed on da ground more dan a second.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"os-yp-after-winter-two-apr-1-nov-15-2028-1\">After winter two (Apr 1 – Nov 15, 2028)</h3>\n<p>No snow falls, so dis part&#39;s got no tiers. Da drought runs right into summer, da fleet grows anyways, an&#39; da year ends up on da porch.</p>\n<div class=\"os-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sat Apr 8</td><td>1.6&quot; of rain, da first real rain in nine weeks. Da burn ban lifts. Da machines get hosed off anyways, even dough none of &#39;em has touched snow since Feb 12, 2027.</td></tr><tr><td>Fri May 19</td><td>Last day of school. Da 1–0 on da garage wall is still up dere, asterisk an&#39; all.</td></tr><tr><td>Thu Jun 1</td><td>Mark III goes on graph paper at four squares to da inch: twin screws, a 40 cm swath, wheels da same size again. It&#39;s dated 6/1/28 in felt-tip. A drawin&#39; every June 1 is a tradition now, eh.</td></tr><tr><td>Sat Jul 22</td><td>108°F. Nobody goes outside after 11 am.</td></tr><tr><td>Mon Aug 14</td><td>First day of school. A 10-year-old moves into da corner house an&#39; asks about da war. Da founders give two different years.</td></tr><tr><td>Sat Oct 14</td><td>Da newcomer&#39;s brudder or sister, 16 now, finishes da t&#39;ird Mark III at 46 hours of printer time apiece. Da block&#39;s got 8 machines now: 4 Mark I, 1 Mark II, 3 Mark III.</td></tr><tr><td>Wed Nov 1</td><td>First frost, 30°F, gone by 9 am. Two more spools of filament get ordered.</td></tr><tr><td>Wed Nov 15</td><td>Bare, low 41°F, No Ammo, Eh. Out on da porch, da two founders, 13 now, can&#39;t agree on winter two for da 10-year-old. One says it snowed on Jan 21. Da udder says dat was lint from da gym vent. Da 10-year-old asks what a spreader is an&#39; gets 40 minutes.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"os-yp-winter-three-nov-21-2028-apr-7-2029-1\">Winter three (Nov 21, 2028 – Apr 7, 2029)</h3>\n<p>Winter three never quits, eh. A cold trough sets up over da east half of da country in mid-November an&#39; sits dere till late March, sendin&#39; a clipper or a storm t&#39;rough every four or five days. Twenty-four snowfalls add up to 76.35&quot;, about nine times what Tulsa usually gets. Six of &#39;em are big storms of 4&quot; or more, an&#39; da five in midwinter each beat da last one, up to 14.0&quot; on Friday, February 16, 2029. Da ground stays white for 112 days. All of it&#39;s Snowball snow, an&#39; every event gets scored on its own, so a clipper on top of two feet of pack still only scores its own inches.</p>\n<div class=\"os-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Snow</th><th>Tier</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tue Nov 21</td><td>0.62&quot;, 5–9 am, 31°F</td><td>Sneakin&#39; Round Da Side</td><td>First snow in 21 months, two days before T&#39;anksgiving. About 18 rounds a side. Da 10-year-old circles 60 feet in 11 seconds an&#39; gets to 12 feet before anybody looks. It works once. Gone by 2 pm.</td></tr><tr><td>Mon Nov 27</td><td>1.4&quot; overnight, 28°F</td><td>Goin&#39; All Afternoon</td><td>Two-hour late start. After school, 80 rounds a side outlast da gloves. Everybody dries out inside at da 40-minute mark an&#39; goes right back out.</td></tr><tr><td>Fri Dec 1</td><td>1.2&quot;, 3–8 am, 27°F</td><td>First Real Snowman</td><td>Da ground stays white from today till March 22. A 22-inch snowman goes up before da bus, an&#39; by noon somebody&#39;s scratched da spreader mark into its back.</td></tr><tr><td>Tue Dec 5</td><td>1.6&quot; overnight, 25°F</td><td>Spreaders Wit&#39; Bodyguards</td><td>Nobody forgot da gravel. Every Mark III goes out wit&#39; two guards, an&#39; finished resurfacin&#39; runs go from under half to nine outta ten.</td></tr><tr><td>Sat Dec 9</td><td>4.4&quot;, 9 pm Fri – 11 am Sat, 24°F</td><td>Da Star Fort</td><td>Da first big storm. A 12-foot square fort gets a pointed bastion on every corner, walls 3 feet high an&#39; 16 inches t&#39;ick. Four of da nine builders argue da angles wit&#39; a protractor till dark. Nobody rushes it twice.</td></tr><tr><td>Wed Dec 13</td><td>2.05&quot; overnight, 23°F</td><td>Diggin&#39; Up Da Angels</td><td>A row of six angels on da side lawn gets cut up overnight for 41 rounds. Da makers file a formal objection, an&#39; dey pass a rule dat no angel gets dug up till it&#39;s a day old. Nobody can check.</td></tr><tr><td>Sun Dec 17</td><td>1.38&quot;, 1–6 pm, 26°F</td><td>Angel Truce, Eh</td><td>Nobody gets t&#39;rown at while dey&#39;re makin&#39; an angel. Nine angels in two hours. Da rule ends at 4 pm when one maker turns out to have been restin&#39; in an angel for 22 minutes.</td></tr><tr><td>Thu Dec 21</td><td>6.0&quot;, 4 pm Wed – 10 am Thu, 22°F</td><td>Whole Buncha Forts</td><td>First day of winter break. T&#39;ree forts hold 400 feet of frontage across four yards, 30 kids in all. Two of &#39;em team up against da t&#39;ird by 1 pm, an&#39; dat lasts nine days.</td></tr><tr><td>Wed Dec 27</td><td>2.5&quot; overnight, 20°F</td><td>Buildin&#39; Forts</td><td>Da Dec 21 forts have glazed over an&#39; slumped. T&#39;ree new U-forts go up on da standard pattern, 30-inch walls a foot t&#39;ick, 250 rounds inside each, an&#39; 90 minutes of buildin&#39; before da first t&#39;row.</td></tr><tr><td>Mon Jan 1</td><td>1.8&quot;, 2–8 am, 19°F</td><td>Skim Coat on Da Works</td><td>New Year&#39;s Day. All 8 machines lay a 2 mm coat over every grey wall on da block. It don&#39;t add nuttin&#39; structural, but from 40 feet everyt&#39;ing looks brand new.</td></tr><tr><td>Fri Jan 5</td><td>8.2&quot;, 7 pm Thu – 1 pm Fri, 21°F</td><td>Tunnels</td><td>Schools close. Da trenches get linked underground, 3 feet down an&#39; 150 feet in all, wit&#39; four entrances, two of &#39;em hidden. Da parents get told once it&#39;s done an&#39; shore it up wit&#39; sled slats every 6 feet.</td></tr><tr><td>Wed Jan 10</td><td>2.2&quot; overnight, 24°F</td><td>Bat an&#39; Racquet</td><td>A plastic bat an&#39; a warped racquet come outta somebody&#39;s garage. A clean hit goes 65 feet, but t&#39;ree rounds outta five bust on da swing, so da packin&#39; gets harder.</td></tr><tr><td>Mon Jan 15</td><td>2.6&quot;, 4–11 am, 22°F</td><td>Spreader Bay in Da Fort</td><td>MLK Day. Every fort gets rebuilt wit&#39; a bay in back, sized for a Mark III wit&#39; da wings folded an&#39; a sled for a roof. Turnaround drops under 3 minutes.</td></tr><tr><td>Sat Jan 20</td><td>1.9&quot; overnight, 18°F</td><td>Dug-In Line</td><td>Two lines get scraped 8 inches into da pack, 20 feet long, facin&#39; each udder at 50 feet. Neider one gets abandoned all afternoon.</td></tr><tr><td>Thu Jan 25</td><td>10.4&quot;, 3 am Thu – 4 am Fri, 20°F</td><td>Bailey Snowmen, Eh</td><td>Schools close T&#39;ursday an&#39; Friday. Da two allied forts share a keep an&#39; a 60-foot inner wall, an&#39; 14 snowmen, 7 feet tall, go up in da bailey where nuttin&#39; can reach &#39;em. Deir carrots get swapped every nine days off a list pinned by da door.</td></tr><tr><td>Tue Jan 30</td><td>2.75&quot; overnight, 23°F</td><td>Da Redoubt</td><td>A fallback spot, 6 feet across wit&#39; 80 rounds, goes up behind each main wall. Bot&#39; get used twice, an&#39; bot&#39; lines get won back inside ten minutes.</td></tr><tr><td>Mon Feb 5</td><td>3.0&quot;, 6 am – 2 pm, 25°F</td><td>Two Forts, Eh</td><td>Early release. Da t&#39;ird fort&#39;s side builds a rival square fort 18 feet on a side, 50 feet from da allied front. Fourteen person-hours of buildin&#39; before anybody t&#39;rows.</td></tr><tr><td>Fri Feb 9</td><td>1.5&quot; overnight, 26°F</td><td>Saucer Races, Ya Know</td><td>A blue saucer shows up to race da green one. Blue wins seven outta twelve over 34 feet, an&#39; somebody&#39;s waitin&#39; at da top all da time.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fri Feb 16</strong></td><td><strong>14.0&quot;, first flakes 5:10 pm Thu, ends 11:30 am Fri, 22°F, wind under 10 mph</strong></td><td><strong>Counter-Battery (da peak)</strong></td><td>Schools close. By 8 am da first catapult&#39;s built outta a sled, a fence post an&#39; four bungee cords: 110 feet, den it breaks on da second shot. By 11 am an inner-tube ballista puts one round in four inside a two-foot circle at 130 feet. By 2 pm dey&#39;re buildin&#39; engines just to knock out da udder engines, out to 140 feet. Four are in service across t&#39;ree forts, 34 kids, an&#39; all 8 machines resurfacin&#39; behind da walls. At dark, 6:12 pm, every fort&#39;s still standin&#39; an&#39; two engines are down.</td></tr><tr><td>Thu Feb 22</td><td>1.0&quot;, 5–9 am, 27°F</td><td>Free-For-All, Eh</td><td>T&#39;ree of da four engines get knocked out inside da week, two by udder engines. Wit&#39; no engines left, nine kids go at it for 15 minutes wit&#39; no teams, an&#39; nobody agrees who won.</td></tr><tr><td>Fri Mar 2</td><td>0.4&quot;, 6–8 am, 31°F</td><td>A Handful, Yah</td><td>Da pack&#39;s down to 14 inches an&#39; crusted. Fresh snow gets rationed to seven rounds carried in one mitten.</td></tr><tr><td>Thu Mar 15</td><td>4.05&quot;, 8 pm Wed – 9 am Thu, 29°F</td><td>Two-Room Fort</td><td>A late big storm. Da last fort of da winter gets a firin&#39; room in front an&#39; a store room in back wit&#39; a doorway between, da first inside wall in da whole war. Arguments get settled in da back room.</td></tr><tr><td>Mon Mar 26</td><td>1.1&quot;, 4–7 am, 32°F</td><td>Snowball Fight, Eh</td><td>Da ground went bare on Mar 23. Eight kids fight from behind parked cars for 25 minutes, 40 rounds a side, an&#39; it&#39;s gone by supper.</td></tr><tr><td>Sat Apr 7</td><td>0.3&quot;, 5–6 am, 33°F</td><td>Two Snowballs, Eh</td><td>Da last snow of da winter, on da back of a car. Two rounds, two t&#39;rows, one hit. Da whole t&#39;ing lasts four seconds.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"os-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Measure</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Snowfalls</td><td>24 (2 Nov, 7 Dec, 7 Jan, 4 Feb, 3 Mar, 1 Apr)</td></tr><tr><td>Big storms (4&quot; or more)</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>Season total</td><td>76.35&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Biggest event</td><td>14.0&quot; on Fri Feb 16 (Counter-Battery)</td></tr><tr><td>Second biggest</td><td>10.4&quot; on Thu Jan 25 (Bailey Snowmen, Eh)</td></tr><tr><td>T&#39;ird biggest</td><td>8.2&quot; on Fri Jan 5 (Tunnels)</td></tr><tr><td>Deepest on da ground</td><td>27&quot; on Feb 16</td></tr><tr><td>Days wit&#39; snow on da ground</td><td>112 in a row (Dec 1 – Mar 22)</td></tr><tr><td>School snow days</td><td>13</td></tr><tr><td>Kids at da peak</td><td>34</td></tr><tr><td>Machines</td><td>8 (4 Mark I, 1 Mark II, 3 Mark III)</td></tr><tr><td>Engines at da peak</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>Peak</td><td>Friday, February 16, 2029</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"os-yp-after-winter-three-apr-8-nov-15-2029-1\">After winter three (Apr 8 – Nov 15, 2029)</h3>\n<p>Dat 0.3&quot; on April 7 was da last snow of winter three, eh. Every day after it scores No Ammo, so dis part&#39;s got no tiers. It&#39;s da year da war gets cleaned up, ruled on, drawn again, an&#39; handed down.</p>\n<div class=\"os-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sun Apr 8</td><td>Da carrot list comes down off da keep door. Da 14 bailey snowmen went t&#39;rough six changes, 98 carrots in all, an&#39; da list goes in da desk drawer wit&#39; bot&#39; drawin&#39;s.</td></tr><tr><td>Sat Apr 14</td><td>Four lawns got brown rectangles where da forts sat for 112 days. One parent reseeds for $63. A sled slat from da tunnels shows up in a downspout.</td></tr><tr><td>Sat Apr 28</td><td>Da garage panel sits on engines for da first time. Da bungee cords go back to whoever owns &#39;em an&#39; da fence post goes back in da fence. Nobody can claim da design, &#39;cause nobody drew one, so da panel rules dat an engine wit&#39; no drawin&#39; belongs to nobody.</td></tr><tr><td>Fri May 18</td><td>Last day of school. Da 1–0 on da garage wall&#39;s got a second line under it now: &quot;W3: 4 engines, 0 winners.&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Sat May 19</td><td>Da newcomer&#39;s brudder or sister, da one wit&#39; da printer, graduates an&#39; heads off to college in August.</td></tr><tr><td>Fri Jun 1</td><td>Da June 1 drawin&#39; is two sheets dis year. One&#39;s Mark IV, a spreader dat folds up to fit on a sled. Da udder&#39;s da war&#39;s first engine drawin&#39;, a counterweight trebuchet wit&#39; every inch marked, dated 6/1/29. Nobody builds it.</td></tr><tr><td>Wed Jul 4</td><td>101°F. Everybody watches da fireworks from where da keep used to be.</td></tr><tr><td>Sat Aug 11</td><td>Da printer moves into da founders&#39; garage wit&#39; a note taped on: &quot;Filament in da blue bin. Don&#39;t print da treb in PLA.&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Mon Aug 13</td><td>First day of school. Da founders start nint&#39; grade, an&#39; da kid who moved into da corner house in 2028, 11 now, starts middle school.</td></tr><tr><td>Thu Nov 1</td><td>First frost, 29°F, gone by 10 am. Da trebuchet drawin&#39; comes outta da drawer for da first time since June.</td></tr><tr><td>Thu Nov 15</td><td>Bare, low 40°F, No Ammo, Eh. Out on da porch, da two founders, 14 now, can&#39;t agree on winter three. One says 34 kids an&#39; four engines. Da udder says 60 kids an&#39; a trebuchet. Da 11-year-old, who was dere for every bit of it, gets it right an&#39; gets told dey were too young to remember.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n"};
